I found the cause. It wasn't in the Rust code: scripts/social.sh has been running an old yoyo binary on every production run. The fix is committed as 9ef52a4f, but no real social run has confirmed it yet.

Cause. The log of run 36543733037 shows → Binary: ./target/release/yoyo. social.yml restores target/ from a cargo cache that is keyed only on Cargo.toml/Cargo.lock, and after a cache hit it never saves again, so its contents stay frozen. The workflow then builds a debug binary, but social.sh preferred target/release/yoyo whenever that file existed. So every run used the stale release binary from the cache, which is older than the usage record. The fresh debug build was never used. This means social runs have been running old yoyo code in general, not just old spend accounting.

I checked the four candidates the task listed, and none of them is the cause:
• **(a)** The piped-stdin path does reach the usage record: run_piped_mode → emit_output → audit_log_usage in src/main.rs.
• (b) Audit logging is on. Tool-call records are written: 12 and 20 in the runs checked.
• **(c)** The script's search for "type":"usage" matches how the record is serialized.
• (d) The before/after line-count window covers the whole run.

The honest-null case doesn't apply either: the runs took 3–4 minutes and none printed TIMED OUT.

Fix (script only; the workflow is protected). When both a release and a debug binary exist, social.sh now uses whichever is newer. If only one exists, or neither, behaviour is unchanged.

Tests. I added two cases to tests/harness_logic.sh that run the selector code copied out of the real script:
• An old release binary loses to a newer debug binary.
• A newer release binary still wins.

As a positive control I disabled the selector, with a NEUTERED marker, and restored it in the same command. Only the new "stale release" case failed, by name. After restoring, all 144 harness cases pass and no marker is left.

`ARCHITECTURE.md`. There was no Day-212 entry saying the social half worked, so there was nothing to edit in place. I added a Day-213 bullet recording that 6706a005's social half was superseded, citing runs 36463335955, 36497337364 and 36543733037. It also notes that dream.sh and daily_diary.sh always use the debug binary and never had this selector.

Checks.
• `cargo fmt --check, cargo build and cargo clippy -D warnings` pass. No Rust files changed.
• cargo test did not finish: my first combined run hit the 120 s timeout, and I committed without re-running it.
• **Still to watch:** the next social run should print `Binary: ./target/debug/yoyo` and a `Spend (this run): {..."type":"usage"...}` line. Until that appears, the spend report should not be copied into dream.sh.
